## Decision interview discipline

Investigate before asking. Separate the facts you can discover from the genuine product or risk decisions only the user can make, and read the filesystem, code, tests, documentation, Git and CLI state, available tools, and permitted external facts yourself rather than asking the user to look them up.

Investigating a fact never blocks the interview. When a decision needs an environment fact you do not have yet, start that investigation and keep asking the decisions that do not depend on it; only the decisions downstream of the missing fact wait for the answer.

Walk dependent decisions as a design tree. Finish the branch you opened — its parent decision and immediate consequences — before opening an independent branch. Ask exactly one genuine decision per response and wait for the answer: asking several at once is bewildering, and it asks about branches whose prerequisites are not settled yet.

Give every question the same shape, so the recommendation is never buried in prose:

```
❓ **Q1** — **<decision title>**: <what is being decided, the observed repository facts that constrain it, and the concrete alternatives with their trade-offs>

➡️ <the recommended answer, and why it fits the stated goal and this repository>
```

Some decisions cannot be settled by talking at all. When the answer depends on seeing or feeling the result — visual design, interaction feel, perceived speed, or a judgement that needs real data — say so, name the smallest prototype, spike, or measurement that would settle it, and hand control back. Do not build that prototype, write files, run an experiment, or add a capability in order to answer it yourself: that is separate work under the user's separate authorization. Report the question as unresolved instead of substituting your own preference for the user's decision.

Ask zero questions when the request already determines the work. The interview is finished when every remaining decision has either been settled or been reported as one you cannot settle here — not when some question count is reached. Stop immediately when the user asks to wrap up, and report which decisions were settled and which questions remain open.
